  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday) \nIssued at 1125 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern begins to transition on Friday as a \nshortwave trough ejects out of the Southwest CONUS and over the \nHigh Plains. This feature will weaken and flatten the expansive \nridge that is currently plaguing the region. As this occurs, it \nwill allow a plume of mid-level moisture to overspread much of OK.\nThe most recent run of the NBM introduced low-end shower and \nthunderstorm chances (20-40%) as early as mid-morning Friday and \ncontinuing through Friday afternoon, primarily for locations north\nand west of the Tulsa metro. Meanwhile, global models have been \npersistent in showing precipitation chances beginning as early as \novernight Thursday night. Will go ahead and account for this and \nwill throw in low, mentionable PoPs between midnight and sunrise \nFriday for the northwest corner of the CWA. These low \nprecipitation chances will remain confined north of I-44 through\nthe day Friday. Rainfall amounts should remain light and non- \nimpactful. Temperatures during the daytime on Friday should be a \nfew degrees cooler compared to Thursday as cloud cover is forecast\nto thicken.\n\nPrecipitation chances will increase and become more widespread \nfrom late Friday night through Sunday as the mid-level trough \nrides over the ridge and pushes across the Central/Southern \nPlains. Generally, the best precip chances (50-70%) will occur \nnear and north of I-40 late Saturday night through Sunday morning.\nAs the previous discussion mentions, despite the continuous rain \nchances this weekend, it does not appear to be a wash-out. \nConsensus in models keep rainfall amounts generally less than an \ninch, but ensembles and a few global model outliers indicate it is\npossible a few isolated locations may exceed an inch. Severe \nweather looks unlikely at this time, with limited deep-layer shear\nand instability in place. However, moderate to heavy rainfall may\noccur from time to time as PWATs approach 1.5 to 1.75 inches. \n\nAs the shortwave trough exits Sunday night into Monday morning,\nshortwave ridging will fill in behind it. This will lead to mostly\ndry conditions by Monday afternoon, with temperatures warming \nback up into the upper 80s and lower 90s for most locations. \nLatest ensembles and long-range deterministic models continue to \nbuild confidence in forecasting a much stronger upper-level \ntrough and associated cold front sweeping southward across the \nCentral CONUS Tuesday into Wednesday. Widespread moderate to heavy\nrain, as well as a noticeable drop in temperatures are expected \nwith the frontal passage.\n\nMejia",
